{{- /* ~/.ssh/config — the SKELETON only. It carries no host, no key, and no
     account: every one of those is identity-bearing and lives in a private
     overlay's fragment under ~/.ssh/config.d/, which this file includes.

     A machine therefore gets exactly the fragments its overlays supply. A
     work-only box has no personal aliases, a personal-only box has no work
     ones, and an ephemeral box has neither — with no conditionals here,
     because a glob that matches nothing is not an error (verified: `ssh -G`
     exits 0 with the skeleton alone).

     ORDERING IS SEMANTIC, and backwards from git. ssh_config says "for each
     parameter, the FIRST obtained value will be used", so an earlier
     fragment wins, whereas ~/.gitconfig's later include wins. The fragments
     are numbered to make that explicit: 10-personal before 20-work, so
     personal takes precedence on any host both of them describe. The Include
     sits above `Host *` for the same reason — a catch-all read first would
     shadow every fragment. tests/ssh-config.bats pins both facts. */ -}}
Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*.conf
{{ if eq .machineClass "mac" }}
# The agent every key in this setup actually lives in. Not a secret and not
# an identity — a fixed path inside 1Password's group container, and the one
# thing here that is the same on every mac.
#
# macOS only, and that matters more than it looks: IdentityAgent OVERRIDES
# $SSH_AUTH_SOCK. Pointing it at this path on a Linux box — where 1Password
# does not run — would make ssh ignore a forwarded agent and fail with no
# usable key, which is precisely how git reaches GitHub from the shared
# workstations.
Host *
	IdentityAgent "~/Library/Group Containers/2BUA8C4S2C.com.1password/t/agent.sock"
{{- end }}
